Long ago, two villages stood on opposite banks of a mighty river. The North Village possessed abundant grain but no water in the dry season. The South Village had the river's blessing but rocky soil that yielded little food.

One year, drought came. The North Village elder approached the river with an offering: half their grain stores, asking the river to carry it south. "If the South Village receives this gift," she declared, "perhaps they will share the river's water when we have need."

The river swept the grain downstream, but along the way, doubt whispered through the currents. "What if they keep it all? What if they forget our generosity?" By the time the grain reached the South Village, the river had held back a portion, hiding it in eddies and pools.

The South Village, seeing the reduced gift, grew suspicious. "They send so little—perhaps they mock us." They returned only a trickle of water upstream.

The North Village elder wept, not knowing the river had betrayed both villages. The river, trying to protect each side from the other's possible betrayal, had created the very mistrust it feared.

To this day, the river runs divided, and neither village knows the truth of what was lost.
